 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF) Mesh Extraction](3692-gaussian-opacity-fields-gof-mesh-extraction.md)
-A method to extract a 3-D triangulated mesh from a trained Gaussian model without retraining, by building a volumetric density field and extracting an isosurface [3692-gaussian-opacity-fields-gof-mesh-extraction.md]. It builds on [Surface and Mesh Extraction](8498-surface-and-mesh-extraction.md) by treating the Gaussian opacity field as a continuous volumetric density function, distinct from sibling methods that rely on distance fields or direct point-cloud reconstruction.
+Extracts a 3-D triangulated mesh from a trained model without retraining by building a volumetric density field and extracting an isosurface. It sets itself apart by relying on density thresholding rather than explicit geometric back-projection or distance-field regularization. Choose it when a training-free, continuous density representation is sufficient.
 
 ### [Distance-based Gaussian Splatting](7411-distance-based-gaussian-splatting.md)
-Distance-based Gaussian Splatting is a method that integrates a pre-existing 3D mesh with 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) primitives to improve rendering quality and geometric consistency in large-scale scenes. It solves the problem of redundant or distorted Gaussian splats in regions where the underlying mesh is inaccurate or overly dense by classifying splats as "tightly-bound" or "loosely-bound" based on their distance to the mesh surface. Unlike standard 3DGS which treats all primitives equally, this method masks occluded splats, prunes those hidden behind the mesh, and aligns tightly-bound splats to the mesh geometry, thereby reducing memory usage and preventing artifacts from mesh reconstruction errors [7411-distance-based-gaussian-splatting.md].
+Integrates a pre-existing 3D mesh with 3DGS primitives to improve rendering quality and geometric consistency in large-scale scenes. It sets itself apart by classifying splats as tightly-bound or loosely-bound based on mesh distance, masking occluded splats, and pruning redundant ones. Choose it when initializing splats on a high-resolution mesh to reduce memory usage and align geometry.
 
 ### [2D-Lift-3D Scene Segmentation](11513-2d-lift-3d-scene-segmentation.md)
-This method segments 3D Gaussian primitives by lifting 2D segmentation masks, generated from a reference view using foundation models like SAM, into 3D space via multi-view label voting [11513-2d-lift-3d-scene-segmentation.md]. It enables partial scene editing (PSE) by decomposing a scene into distinct Gaussian groups, allowing independent manipulation of color, opacity, and lighting [11513-2d-lift-3d-scene-segmentation.md]. Unlike methods that require learnable feature vectors or extensive training for segmentation [11513-2d-lift-3d-scene-segmentation.md], this approach is training-free and relies on geometric projection and voting, though it may require iterative refinement for complex scenes [11513-2d-lift-3d-scene-segmentation.md].
+Segments 3D Gaussian primitives by lifting 2D segmentation masks from a reference view into 3D space via multi-view label voting. It sets itself apart by being training-free and relying on foundation model masks rather than learnable feature vectors. Choose it for partial scene editing without modifying the base scene representation.
 
 ### [Mesh Extraction via Poisson Reconstruction](12790-mesh-extraction-via-poisson-reconstruction.md)
-This method extracts a textured triangular mesh from an optimized 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) scene by converting rendered 2D depth and normal maps into a 3D oriented point cloud, which is then processed by a Poisson surface reconstruction algorithm [12790-mesh-extraction-via-poisson-reconstruction.md][12790-mesh-extraction-via-poisson-reconstruction.md]. It differs from direct meshing approaches like Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF) [3692-gaussian-opacity-fields-gof-mesh-extraction.md] or distance-based methods [7411-distance-based-gaussian-splatting.md] by relying on post-processing rendered geometric cues rather than modifying the core Gaussian representation or optimizing a signed distance field [12790-mesh-extraction-via-poisson-reconstruction.md][12790-mesh-extraction-via-poisson-reconstruction.md].
+Extracts a textured triangular mesh by converting rendered 2D depth and normal maps into a 3D oriented point cloud, which is then reconstructed via Poisson surface reconstruction. It sets itself apart by relying on explicit geometric back-projection rather than density thresholding or distance-field regularization. Choose it when explicit normal consistency and point-cloud-based reconstruction are preferred.
 
 ## Background
-The extraction of meshes from 3DGS is a necessary step for integrating neural radiance fields and Gaussian splats into traditional computer graphics pipelines. Early 3DGS implementations focused solely on novel view synthesis [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md). Subsequent research identified the lack of explicit geometry as a limitation for applications requiring collision detection or texture mapping. Methods like GOF [3692-gaussian-opacity-fields-gof-mesh-extraction.md] and Poisson-based extraction [12790-mesh-extraction-via-poisson-reconstruction.md] emerged to bridge this gap, while distance-based approaches [7411-distance-based-gaussian-splatting.md] addressed geometric consistency in large-scale scenes.
